Step‐by‐Step Reasoning

1. The mission requires a robot (or multiple robots) to perform two main phases: collection and delivery. In the collection phase the robot must navigate to one or more storages, send a message to request the resource, wait for retrieval, and then physically collect the resource. In the delivery phase the robot must navigate to the delivery location and make one or more runs to deliver all resources.  
2. In both phases battery level is monitored. In the collection phase if the battery falls below 10% the robot must go to the recharging station and reassign the task. In the delivery phase if the battery falls below 30% the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining delivery. If the resource is not returned to the checkpoint an alert is triggered and a report sent to the sector manager.  
3. When multiple items must be collected from different storages, multiple robots can be assigned in parallel to reduce the overall mission time.  
4. Based on these observations we decompose the mission into a root goal (Mission Execution) with three main branches: Collection Phase, Delivery Phase and Multi‐Robot Coordination.  
5. The Collection Phase is further split into “Resource Retrieval” (which itself is a sequence of navigation, message sending, waiting and retrieval) and “Battery Monitoring in Collection.”  
6. The Delivery Phase is split into “Delivery Execution” (navigation, delivery, possibly repeating runs) and “Battery Monitoring in Delivery.” To cover the possibility that battery monitoring fails (i.e. the resource is not returned to the checkpoint) we use a fallback structure: if battery monitoring fails then the Alert Handling branch is executed.  
7. Finally, the Multi‐Robot Coordination goal covers the case when multiple storages are involved; its tasks (identifying storages and assigning robots) are executed in parallel.

